      "citations": null,
      "text": "The bug is that `open(settings_path, \"r\")` raises `FileNotFoundError` when `~/.qiskit/settings.conf` doesn't exist. Installing Qiskit does not create this file or its folder; the user has to create them. The fix creates the `.qiskit` directory and the settings file if they are missing. It writes the `circuit_drawer = mpl` setting I believe the textbook asks for; the question doesn't quote the exact contents. After that, the file is read and printed as before.\n\n```python\n\nimport os\n\nhome = os.path.expanduser(\"~\")\nsettings_path = os.path.join(home, \".qiskit\", \"settings.conf\")\n\n# FIX: file/folder may not exist (Qiskit doesn't create them) -> create ~/.qiskit and a default settings.conf if missing, because opening a nonexistent file for reading raises FileNotFoundError\nos.makedirs(os.path.dirname(settings_path), exist_ok=True)\nif not os.path.exists(settings_path):\n    with open(settings_path, \"w\") as f:\n        f.write(\"[default]\\ncircuit_drawer = mpl\\n\")\n\nwith open(settings_path, \"r\") as f:\n    contents = f.read()\n\nprint(contents)\n\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
